pr_comments:
["Remember that the target of a function application can be any expression,  including `a.b` or `new A` or another function application.
", "Yeah, I think you need a few more tests with other patterns to make sure this rule acts as you think it should:

``` js
foo.bar();
var x = new Foo();
(function() {

}());
```
", "Ah thanks for catching these cases @michaelficarra @nzakas, putting in code and tests now
", "Can you rebase?
", "Look like Travis wasn't doing so hot with the npm outage. Passed on 0.10, is there another way to kick off a Travis job for the other Node versions?
", "I don't know of a way. I'm just going to merge in.
", "The only thing I would have liked in addition to this is a test that `new Foo` passes. Looking at the code, it appears that it would.
", "Hmm. It looks like `foo( (0))` would fail since _all_ opening parens are checked for preceding spaces, instead of just the parens that are part of the function application.
", "@michaelficarra doh didn't think about that case, good catch - opened https://github.com/nzakas/eslint/issues/353. I'll open a PR with the fix and another with the additional test coverage.
"]

