", "@mdjermanovic @nzakas ", "Hi @nopeless, please avoid pinging team members for review, it creates unnecessary spam in their notifications :( 

Once you open a pr/issue we will definitely look into it 👍 .

Once your pr is ready for review move it from `draft` to `open`, this will request the team for review.", "Thanks for the PR! Few notes about functionalities:

> * Allow void in `no-promise-executor`

This should be behind an option, for example `"allowVoid": boolean`. The option should also allow `return void`.

> * Add auto fix that adds a `void` in front of the returned expression (open for debate)

I think this rule shouldn't provide autofixes. Generally, rules that relate to possible logic errors in code should not be auto-fixable. For example, `new Promise(() => 1);` looks like a logic error. Automatically fixing that code to `new Promise(() => void 1);` would hide the error.



", "> Thanks for the PR! Few notes about functionalities:
> 
> > * Allow void in `no-promise-executor`
> 
> This should be behind an option, for example `"allowVoid": boolean`. The option should also allow `return void`.
> 
> > * Add auto fix that adds a `void` in front of the returned expression (open for debate)
> 
> I think this rule shouldn't provide autofixes. Generally, rules that relate to possible logic errors in code should not be auto-fixable. For example, `new Promise(() => 1);` looks like a logic error. Automatically fixing that code to `new Promise(() => void 1);` would hide the error.

Understandable, what should `allowVoid`'s default be?", "> Understandable, what should `allowVoid`'s default be?

`false`, to retain the current behavior of this rule.", "@mdjermanovic I have ran into an edge case that doesn't seem to have a good solution to

Currently, there are two suggestions avaliable: prepend `void`, wrap in `{}`

The second suggestion seems to produce a syntax error because of, in my opinion, a perculiarity in eslint's AST

```js
() => (1);
// structure looks like
const ast = {
  type: "ArrowFunctionExpression",
  body: {
    type: "Literal",
    raw: "1"
  }
}
```

So, wrapping `{}` around the arrow body doesn't result in `{(1)}`, but `({1})`, which is obviously a syntax error.

What should I do?", "> So, wrapping `{}` around the arrow body doesn't result in `{(1)}`, but `({1})`, which is obviously a syntax error.

Yes, parentheses around node are not part of the node in AST. Instead of wrapping `node.body`, we could add `{` before the first token after `=>`, and `}` after the last token of the arrow expression node. Something like this:

```js
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Requirements
//------------------------------------------------------------------------------

//... 

const astUtils = require("./utils/ast-utils");
```

```js
fix(fixer) {
    const arrowToken = sourceCode.getTokenBefore(node.body, astUtils.isArrowToken);
    const firstToken = sourceCode.getTokenAfter(arrowToken);
    const lastToken = sourceCode.getLastToken(node);

    return [
        fixer.insertTextBefore(firstToken, "{"),
        fixer.insertTextAfter(lastToken, "}")
    ];
}
```

