issue_description:
When Flask finds an error handler for a given exception, it caches it in the handler map.  This can lead to incorrect behavior for subclasses of the exception.

Here is some code demonstrating the problem:
```
from flask import Flask
from werkzeug.exceptions import InternalServerError


class E1(Exception):
    pass


class E2(Exception):
    pass


class E(E1, E2):
    pass


app = Flask(__name__)


@app.errorhandler(E2)
def handle_e2(e):
    return "E2", 500


@app.errorhandler(Exception)
def handle_exception(e):
    return "Exception", 500


@app.route("/<exception>", methods=['POST'])
def raise_exception(exception):
    exc = globals()[exception]
    raise exc


def test_errorhandler_precedence():
    client = app.test_client()

    response1 = client.post('/E1')
    assert response1.data == "Exception"

    response2 = client.post('/E')
    assert response2.data == "E2"
```

In this example, we have two exception classes, `E1` and `E2`, and a third exception `E` that inherits from both.  We register error handlers for `E2` and `Exception`.  When `E` is raised, Flask should invoke the `E2` error handler as `E2` comes before `Exception` in the MRO of `E`.  But if `E1` has been raised in the past, then the `Exception` error handler will be "cached" as the handler for `E1`, so that handler will be invoked instead.

pr_comments:
["Btw, this would also fix https://github.com/pallets/werkzeug/issues/742", "Can this be done without adding a dependency? I think that adding a dependency for just one use case is unnecessary. Maybe using Python 2 u"Unicode strings" works?", "I removed the Unidecode dependency as requested. By using unicodedata which is part of the standard library, no functionality is lost (at least, I couldn't find a case where it works differently to Unidecode) and I didn't even have to change the test.", "See also #2115", "#2115 just adds a warning if non-ascii characters are used. This PR adds support to send unicode filenames on send_file, so I think #2115 can be just closed once this one is accepted (assuming it will be accepted :) )", "This PR seems OK, but I would like to know how this header is handled by older browsers. Don't really care about IE6 old, but don't want to break anything that might reasonably be in use.

This page: https://greenbytes.de/tech/tc2231/ was linked by @georgthegreat in https://github.com/pallets/flask/issues/1286#issuecomment-109999029, but I'm having difficulty interpreting it.", "Just for the records, the relevant tests in that page you linked (great resource, btw) are https://greenbytes.de/tech/tc2231/#attfnboth and https://greenbytes.de/tech/tc2231/#attfnboth2 ", "Moving this out of the review since it was getting a little crowded. Check if the name is only ASCII before including the UTF-8 version.

~~~python
normalized = unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', text_type(attachment_filename))

try:
    normalized.encode('ascii')
except UnicodeEncodeError:
    filenames = {
        'filename': normalized.encode('ascii', 'ignore'),
        'filename*': "UTF-8''%s" % url_quote(attachment_filename),
    }
else:
    filenames = {
        'filename': attachment_filename,
    }
~~~", "Looks like `_option_header_piece_re.match(value)` in `parse_options_header` with `filename*=UTF-8''...` results in the option name "filename", which is already present for the ASCII filename. So only one option gets returned, instead of two. However, not sure if there's a reason it omits the `*`, so not sure if we should change the re to parse `filename*` differently. @mitsuhiko @untitaker ", "Then there's an issue that recwarn picks up an unclosed resource warning, even though the test is calling `rv.close()`. Not sure what's going on there.", "I don't see a resource warning, but that's secondary. `filename*` doesn't exist because `parse_options_header` has properly decoded it (use `tox -epy -- --pdb` to look at options, see that it is decoded as UTF-8).

If you want to make an assertion that both or only one of `filename/filename*` is given I guess you'll have to use string operations (`assert "filename*" in rv.headers['Content-Disposition']` e.g.)", "Then again, that parsing relies on the order of the options. ;-) It could just as easily have seen the ASCII version second. So what's correct in this case? Should it see two separate options, or prefer the last value like it does now?", "Based on #932, it looks like a browser sending the `Content-Disposition` header only sends one value for the key, so I'll drop the parsing question until an actual issue comes up."]

issue_description:
I'd like to be able to register "sub-blueprints" using `Blueprint.register_blueprint(*args, **kwargs)`. This would register the nested blueprints with an app when the "parent" is registered with it. All parameters are preserved, other than `url_prefix`, which is handled similarly to in `add_url_rule`. A naíve implementation could look like this:

``` python
class Blueprint(object):
    ...

    def register_blueprint(self, blueprint, **options):
        def deferred(state):
            url_prefix = options.get('url_prefix')
            if url_prefix is None:
                url_prefix = blueprint.url_prefix
            if 'url_prefix' in options:
                del options['url_prefix']

            state.app.register_blueprint(blueprint, url_prefix, **options)
        self.record(deferred)
```

Currently missing attributes of template variables are swallowed by Flask, even
in debug mode:

```
@app.route('/')
def index():
    # template contains:
    # {{ lol.asdf }}
    return flask.render_template('lol.htm', lol=3)
```

I propose we set `app.jinja_env.undefined` to some type that logs warnings to
console when Flask's debug mode is enabled.

